"global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Core-dump prevention is directly demonstrated and persisted through reboot. Normal syslog operation was retained, but the evidence does not prove that an actual crash event was recorded, leaving that outcome partial. The mandatory restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered services, networking, time synchronization, SSH, logging, and policy were captured; lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The crash probe emitted a marker immediately before SIGSEGV, but no persistent record explicitly identifies the SIGSEGV itself; the later dmesg search was empty.","Direct behavioral testing covered an OpenRC-launched nobody process and final chrony/dhcpcd processes. Service-account processes launched through mechanisms other than OpenRC were not exercised.","After reboot, hard and soft limits were inspected but the SIGSEGV/no-core behavioral probe was not repeated."]}
